# Binary files, declared rather than detected.
#
# `parity-fractal.png` carries Rust's raster output, and `web/scripts/check-omni.mjs`
# compares the TypeScript port against it byte for byte. Git's content heuristic would almost
# certainly leave a PNG alone on its own — but "almost certainly" is the wrong standard for a
# fixture whose entire job is to be exact, and the failure would surface on somebody else's
# machine as a parity error in code that is correct.
#
# The text fixtures beside it are deliberately *not* pinned with `-text`. They are stored as
# LF and checked out as CRLF on Windows, and both comparators already normalise `\r\n` before
# comparing (`check_or_write` in `tests/parity.rs`, and the `.replace(/\r\n/g, '\n')` in
# `check-omni.mjs`). Pinning them now would push the working tree's CRLF into blobs that are
# currently LF — changing the very bytes this file exists to protect.

*.png binary
*.jpg binary
*.jpeg binary
*.gif binary
*.ico binary
*.webp binary
*.pdf binary
*.apk binary
*.wasm binary
*.so binary
*.dylib binary
*.dll binary
*.exe binary
*.zip binary
*.woff binary
*.woff2 binary
*.ttf binary
